TEXT -680 -408 Left 2 !.tran 0 100m 0m 2u
TEXT -680 -400 Left 2 !.param tdv=1 tdt=1 fn1=50 omega_n={2*pi*fn1}
TEXT -680 -416 Left 2 !.param length=10 fact=1
TEXT -280 -456 Left 2 ;See ATP-file:\nOILcable_400kV_1200mm2_shielded_2Xgrounded_TrianglePlacement_ThinSolidShield.acp
TEXT -712 -912 Left 6 ;Test of sheat currents at thin sheath \nYou see that now the sheath current is \nmuch lower than the conductor current
TEXT -656 -520 Left 4 ;Shield
TEXT -152 -592 Left 2 !.param RL=1e10
TEXT -560 -424 Left 2 ;Current is about 215A if fact=1 (like at ATP Pi-model)\nConcuctor current is about 326A
TEXT -560 -488 Left 2 ;You can play with the fact parameter\n( only for models with uncoupled R's )\nto model higher,smaller shield resistance
TEXT -712 -808 Left 2 ;NOTE: This is true for a line with 1km length (parameter length=1) where the\ncurrent is mainly given by the load. You can also change fact parameter to\ntest higher shield resistance and their influence at the shield current \nBUT: If you set length of cable to 10 the conductor current is only partially \ninfluenced by the load but also from the capacitive charging current of the \ncable which is at some amount ALWAYS also present in the shield\nYou can see: \nParameter fact has more influence on the sheats current if the conductor current\nis mainly fed by the load current and not the charging current of the line
TEXT -400 -680 Left 2 ;Play also with line length to increase charging current
TEXT -712 -712 Left 2 ;Conclusion: \nSince longer high voltage cables (400kV) have high charging currents\n(compared to 20kV cables) these cables needs always crossbonding \nschemes to hold the shield currents low
TEXT -64 -592 Left 2 ;Play with load value!\nRL = 1000\nRL= 400\nRL = 1e10
TEXT -344 -664 Left 2 ;Play with fact to model higher and lower shield resistance
TEXT -760 -512 Left 2 ;You can also add\na short circuit\nimpedance to \nthe system to model\ninrush currents
TEXT -560 -440 Left 2 ;For inrush currents use small timestep
